WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:05.550
If you want to become a billionaire, the most
probable way to get there is starting a company.

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:08.770
Over the years, there have been a couple of
billionaires who were able to amass their

00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:13.090
wealth through diligent investing, making
movies, and even writing books.

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:17.750
But, despite the avenue, nearly all billionaires
can attribute their wealth to some sort of

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:21.880
individual venture whether that be starting
a hedge fund or pursuing directing.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:27.130
There are, however, a select group of individuals
who were able to hit the elusive 10 figure

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:30.189
mark working as employees their entire lives.

00:00:30.189 --> 00:00:35.530
And the first person to achieve this remarkable
feat was a Cuban refugee named Roberto Goizueta.

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:42.089
So, here’s how a Cuban refugee became the
world’s first 9 to 5 billionaire.

00:00:42.089 --> 00:00:47.539
Roberto Goizeuta was born on November 18,
1931 in Havana, Cuba.

00:00:47.539 --> 00:00:51.409
When Roberto was a kid, the family was actually
quite well off.

00:00:51.409 --> 00:00:55.989
His father was an architect and owned a successful
construction/hardware business.

00:00:55.989 --> 00:00:59.930
Meanwhile, his mother was the heir to a large
sugar plantation.

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:04.660
Roberto actually grew up in a mansion that
his grandfather built, so life was pretty

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:05.660
good.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:10.220
He attended local private schools in Havana
during his early days before attending a preparatory

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:13.620
school called Cheshire Academy in New Haven,
Connecticut.

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:17.900
Roberto would end up doing well enough in
school to get accepted into Yale University

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:20.210
where he majored in Chemical Engineering.

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:26.060
Roberto graduated from Yale in 1953, and right
after graduation, Roberto would marry Olga

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:29.100
Casteleiro and move back to Havana.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:34.250
After graduation, his father really wanted
Roberto to join the family business, but Roberto

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:35.780
had other plans.

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:39.960
Roberto wanted to make a name for himself
instead of following in his father’s footsteps.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:43.960
So, he looked around the newspaper when he
found an advertisement from an unidentified

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:47.280
company that was looking for a bilingual chemical
engineer.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:51.180
The company turned out to be Coca Cola and
Roberto would get the job.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:55.220
However, his father only let Roberto take
the job if he agreed to work at the family

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:57.190
company every Saturday.

00:01:57.190 --> 00:02:01.979
Roberto agreed to his father’s condition
and took the job on July 4, 1954.

00:02:01.979 --> 00:02:06.870
The job wasn’t particularly glamorous, but
it did pay $500 per month which is the same

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:09.010
as about $5000 today.

00:02:09.010 --> 00:02:14.230
Apparently, his friends thought he was crazy
for taking a job at Coca-Cola instead of his

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:18.659
father’s company, but soon enough this would
turn out to be the right decision.

00:02:18.659 --> 00:02:22.630
Over the next five years, Roberto quickly
worked up the corporate ladder becoming the

00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:27.579
chief technical director for all five of Coca-Cola’s
bottling plants in Cuba.

00:02:27.579 --> 00:02:30.700
At this point, it seemed like nothing could
go wrong.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:35.190
Roberto had a solid career going, and even
if he lost his job, he had a great family

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:36.599
to back him up.

00:02:36.599 --> 00:02:41.249
But this is when Cuba would experience an
unprecedented turn of events.

00:02:41.249 --> 00:02:47.159
In January of 1959, communist leader Fidel
Castro led a rebellion against the Cuban government,

00:02:47.159 --> 00:02:50.799
and he would successfully overthrow the government
just a month later.

00:02:50.799 --> 00:02:55.349
Fidel Castro instantly named himself the premier
of the country and made it clear that he was

00:02:55.349 --> 00:02:57.840
going to take over all private businesses.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:02.519
Roberto quickly sent his children to stay
with relatives in the US, but Roberto and

00:03:02.519 --> 00:03:06.560
his parents would try to stick it out in Cuba
for as long as possible.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:12.349
Eventually, in August of 1960, the family
decided that it was time to get out, and they

00:03:12.349 --> 00:03:16.519
would use a family vacation to Miami as an
excuse to flee the country.

00:03:16.519 --> 00:03:22.230
When they reached Miami, the family had successfully
escaped communism, but they had lost everything

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:23.800
in the process.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:29.230
The family was staying at a small motel room,
and they only had $40 and 100 shares of Coca

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:30.340
Cola left.

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:35.019
To make things worse, just two months later,
the new Cuban government would seize all of

00:03:35.019 --> 00:03:38.639
Coca Cola’s assets in Cuba as well as the
family’s assets.

00:03:38.639 --> 00:03:41.569
So, there was officially no going back.

00:03:41.569 --> 00:03:47.040
The only silver lining of the situation was
that Coca Cola didn’t lay off or fire Roberto.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:51.450
But given that Coca Cola had lost all of their
assets in Cuba, Roberto had to start again

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:52.599
from the bottom.

00:03:52.599 --> 00:03:58.030
In 1961, Coca Cola moved Roberto to the Bahamas
where he worked as a chemist.

00:03:58.030 --> 00:04:01.049
Given Roberto’s previous experience at Coca
Cola though, it didn’t take him too long

00:04:01.049 --> 00:04:03.529
to work up the corporate ladder once again.

00:04:03.529 --> 00:04:09.199
By 1963, Roberto was promoted to staff assistant
to the senior vice president for Latin America.

00:04:09.199 --> 00:04:14.139
And in 1964, Roberto was transferred to Coca
Cola’s headquarters in Atlanta, Georgia.

00:04:14.139 --> 00:04:17.940
Just two years after that, Roberto was promoted
to vice president of technical research and

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:22.120
development which made him the youngest person
in history to reach that position.

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:24.750
But this was just the beginning.

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:30.190
While Roberto was working as vice president,
the former chairman of Coca Cola, Robert Woodruff

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:33.160
would become quite impressed with Roberto’s
performance.

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:37.630
Woodruff was 77 years old at the time and
he had just retired from Coca Cola a few years

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:41.380
ago, but he still played a crucial role within
the company.

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:45.380
Soon enough, Woodruff and Roberto would become
friends and often ate lunch together.

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:49.480
It’s thought that Woodruff considered Roberto
to be his disciple.

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:54.410
Several years later, in 1974, Roberto was
promoted to senior vice president of the technical

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:58.890
division, and just one year after that, he
was promoted to executive vice president of

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:00.270
the technical division.

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:05.580
Roberto served as one of Coca Cola’s executive
vice presidents till 1979 when the president

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:08.460
of Coca Cola J Lucian Smith resigned.

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:12.120
This allowed Roberto to move up to being a
vice chairman of the company.

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:16.220
At the time, Coca Cola had 6 vice chairmen,
and these guys were considered to be the next

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:18.800
in line for becoming chairman and CEO.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:21.920
However, within a year, the system would be
changed.

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:26.280
4 of the vice chairmen would become executive
vice presidents, and Roberto would take on

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:30.550
three roles which were director, president,
and Chief Operating Officer.

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:34.980
As COO, Roberto eliminated natural sugar from
Coca Cola’s sodas.

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:39.310
Instead, he opted for high-fructose corn syrup
which was 40% cheaper.

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:41.990
This clever business decision further impressed
Woodruff.

00:05:41.990 --> 00:05:47.450
And, just a year later in 1981 when Paul Austin,
the former CEO, retired, Roberto would be

00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:53.020
named the new CEO and chairman of Coca Cola
with a strong recommendation from Woodruff.

00:05:53.020 --> 00:05:55.890
And this is when Roberto would really prove
his ability.

00:05:55.890 --> 00:06:01.400
You see, Coca Cola was doing just fine in
terms of revenue and profits, but a storm

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:03.610
was brewing in the background and the media
picked up on it.

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:07.970
Coca Cola’s sales had flat lined for quite
some time now, and this wasn’t because Coca

00:06:07.970 --> 00:06:10.050
Cola had reached market saturation.

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:13.970
The soft drink market was still growing and
the reason Coca Cola didn’t see any growth

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:19.140
was because Pepsi was eating it all up thanks
to the marketing genius of John Sculley.

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:24.310
Coca Cola was still outselling Pepsi at soda
fountains, but Pepsi had actually overtaken

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:29.300
Coca Cola within the food store market since
1979 and they were quickly building up market

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:30.300
share.

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:35.170
So, Roberto arranged an executive meeting
where he explained his plan for the 1980s.

00:06:35.170 --> 00:06:39.150
The key point he hammered with this meeting
was the need for reinvestment.

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:41.520
In his mind, the idea was super simple.

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:45.240
Borrow money at low interest rates, invest
it in high returning assets and then pocket

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:46.240
the difference.

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:49.830
But, this was a completely foreign idea to
Coca Cola.

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:54.510
Coca Cola had never borrowed money in their
entire history, and this made many executives

00:06:54.510 --> 00:06:56.660
skeptical about Roberto’s plan.

00:06:56.660 --> 00:07:00.320
They all knew that Coca Cola needed major
change to win back market share from Pepsi

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:02.740
though, so they went along with it.

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:07.270
Roberto’s first major decision was the introducing
new drinks, the first of which being Diet

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:09.060
Coke in 1982.

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:14.340
Diet Coke was a massive success and Roberto
would follow this up with Cherry Coke in 1985

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:17.480
which also became a major money maker for
Coca Cola.

00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:19.710
Roberto wasn’t free of mistakes though.

00:07:19.710 --> 00:07:25.080
In 1985, Roberto also oversaw the release
of New Coke which changed the formula of classic

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:26.080
coke.

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:29.480
Though Coca Cola had gathered a significant
amount of objective data that people actually

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:33.410
liked the taste of new Coke better than the
original coke, the market didn't actually

00:07:33.410 --> 00:07:34.670
respond that well.

00:07:34.670 --> 00:07:38.820
There was something about the nostalgia and
familiarity of original coke that even Coca

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:40.990
Cola themselves couldn’t beat.

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:44.520
Roberto didn’t make a fuss over this weird
reaction though.

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:48.950
He quickly listened to the market and brought
back the original coke formula within just

00:07:48.950 --> 00:07:50.090
79 days.

00:07:50.090 --> 00:07:54.380
Aside from experimenting with new drinks,
Roberto completely overhauled Coca Cola’s

00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:55.630
marketing effort.

00:07:55.630 --> 00:07:59.230
Until then, Coca-Cola was extremely passive
with their marketing.

00:07:59.230 --> 00:08:03.750
Roberto changed this by introducing slogans
like Coke is It!, You Can’t Beat the Feeling,

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:05.740
and Always Coca Cola.

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:09.660
This aggressive style carried into the company’s
expansion efforts as well.

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:14.750
For instance, after the Berlin wall fell in
1989, Roberto instantly expanded the company

00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:18.550
in the Eastern Europe despite the terrible
economic condition of the area.

00:08:18.550 --> 00:08:22.470
While this was a questionable move at the
time, Coke overtook Pepsi as the preferred

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:25.180
soft drink in Europe just three years later.

00:08:25.180 --> 00:08:29.160
This aggressive marketing made Coca Cola one
of the most recognized brands in the world.

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:34.599
It’s estimated that 94% of the entire world’s
population recognizes Coke’s red and white

00:08:34.599 --> 00:08:35.620
logo.

00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:38.610
Roberto also spent a lot of money on vertical
integration.

00:08:38.610 --> 00:08:43.450
Throughout the 1980s, Roberto invested into
a variety of different bottling companies

00:08:43.450 --> 00:08:48.450
which allowed Coca Cola to significantly reduce
their operating costs and increase their profits.

00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:53.410
For a bit of time, Roberto also experimented
with investments outside of the beverage industry.

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:58.880
For example, in 1982, Coca Cola bought Columbia
pictures for $692 million.

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:03.930
At first, the investment actually performed
quite poorly, but after the release of Ghostbusters,

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:09.610
Columbia pictures exploded and Roberto was
able to sell the company in 1989 for a $1.5

00:09:09.610 --> 00:09:11.170
billion profit.

00:09:11.170 --> 00:09:16.029
Despite this success though, Roberto decided
that it was best to focus on just one industry.

00:09:16.029 --> 00:09:20.340
Roberto strongly believed that the sole purpose
of every company was to maximize shareholder

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:24.660
returns, and as long as a leader accomplished
this, good things would follow.

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:28.630
In his opinion, the most effective way to
accomplish this was being focused and being

00:09:28.630 --> 00:09:29.630
aggressive.

00:09:29.630 --> 00:09:31.740
And that’s exactly what he did.

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:36.360
Unfortunately though, Roberto’s career would
abruptly come to an end during his peak.

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:41.430
Roberto had been a lifelong cigarette smoker
and in September of 1997, he would be diagnosed

00:09:41.430 --> 00:09:48.590
with Lung Cancer and he would die just one
month later on October 19, 1997 at age 65.

00:09:48.590 --> 00:09:52.500
Despite Roberto’s untimely death, he had
already done wonders for Coca Cola.

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:59.340
By 1996, Coca Cola was outselling Pepsi internationally
by a ratio of 3 to 1, and as you would guess

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:02.170
profits and stock price had gone to the moon.

00:10:02.170 --> 00:10:08.010
When Roberto took over Coca Cola in 1981,
the company was worth a solid $2.8 billion.

00:10:08.010 --> 00:10:12.240
But shortly after his death, Coca Cola was
worth $184 billion.

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:17.460
That’s equivalent to a 64x return within
just 17 years.

00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:22.560
To put that into perspective, Coca Cola stock
has only grown 31% since Roberto’s death.

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:26.180
No wonder Warren Buffet made so much off of
Coca Cola.

00:10:26.180 --> 00:10:30.390
This phenomenal performance has made Roberto
Goizueta one of the most respected and highly

00:10:30.390 --> 00:10:33.000
regarded CEOs of all time.

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:36.520
And many of his business philosophies are
taught in the most prestigious business schools

00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:38.790
around the world today.

00:10:38.790 --> 00:10:42.339
Considering all of this, I don’t think you’d
be surprised to hear that Roberto was worth

00:10:42.339 --> 00:10:48.180
$1.3 billion at the time of his death making
him the world’s first employee billionaire.

